# Set 2 · Q. No. 8(a) · Short Note: Gender and Good Governance
@py pr_question 2 Q8a

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map for the short note: every branch is one headed point.
@note A 10-mark note: define governance and good governance, give four arguments why gender equality is essential to it, show Pakistan's machinery and gaps, and close with a judgement.
@ask Short note: GENDER and GOOD GOVERNANCE — why GENDER EQUALITY is ESSENTIAL to good governance
(1) Governance: World Bank 1992; wider than government
(2) Good governance: UNESCAP's eight characteristics
(3) Four arguments: justice, interests, resource, symbol
(4) The rights basis: UDHR, ICCPR, CEDAW, Constitution
(5) Pakistan: machinery present, power absent
(6) Judgement: no good governance without women
:::

## Decoding the question
A **10-mark note** (about **550–650 words**). Defining governance has **never been asked** — a blind spot. Define **governance** and **good governance** precisely, then answer the "**why**": give reasons, linked to the characteristics of good governance, and apply them to Pakistan.

## The model answer

### 1. Governance

#### i) Definition
The **World Bank** (==1992==) defined governance as "the manner in which **power is exercised** in the management of a country's economic and social resources". The term spread after its ==1989== report on Sub-Saharan Africa spoke of a "**crisis of governance**".

#### ii) Governance versus government
**Government** is the set of institutions holding state power; **governance** is the whole **process** of managing public affairs — including markets, parties, civil society, the media and informal bodies such as ***jirgas***. The **UNDP** (==1997==) described it as the exercise of authority through which citizens **articulate interests, exercise rights, meet obligations and mediate differences**.

### 2. Good governance

#### i) The eight characteristics
**UNESCAP** lists **participation, rule of law, transparency, responsiveness, consensus orientation, equity and inclusiveness, effectiveness and efficiency, and accountability**.

#### ii) Gender is built in
Participation "by **both men and women**" is part of the definition itself: governance that excludes half the population cannot be participatory, equitable or responsive.

::: fig cycle | Gender equality runs through every characteristic of good governance.
@centre Women in governance
Participation: half the citizens included
Equity: laws and budgets serve women too
Responsiveness: women's needs reach the agenda
Accountability: sex-disaggregated data
Rule of law: rights enforced for women
:::

### 3. Why gender equality is essential: four arguments

#### i) Justice
Women are half the citizens; excluding them from decisions is unjust.

#### ii) Interests
Women have distinct experiences — of care, maternity, violence and work — that men may overlook. The **village pump** example shows it: an all-male council places the pump near the market, while the women who fetch water need it near their homes. **Who sits at the table changes what is decided.** **Jane Mansbridge** (==1999==) adds that women's presence matters most where interests are not yet on the agenda — as domestic violence was before women entered parliaments.

#### iii) Resource
Governance that excludes women wastes half the nation's talent.

#### iv) Symbol
Women in power change ideas about who can lead and inspire girls — **Benazir Bhutto** (==1988==) and **Maryam Nawaz** (==2024==).

### 4. The rights basis

#### i) International law
The **UDHR** (Article 21, ==1948==) and the **ICCPR** (Article 25, ==1966==) guarantee the right to take part in government; **CEDAW** Article 7 requires equality in political and public life, and Article 4(1) permits **temporary special measures** such as quotas; **SDG target 5.5** calls for women's equal leadership.

#### ii) The Constitution
Articles **25**, **32** (women in local government) and **34** (full participation of women), and the reserved seats under Articles **51**, **59** and **106**.

### 5. Pakistan: machinery present, power absent

#### i) The machinery
The **Women's Division** (==1979==), the **NCSW** (==2000==; Act ==2012==), the **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women** (==2002==), the **Gender Reform Action Plan**, a **Gender Responsive Budgeting Initiative**, harassment ombudspersons (==2010==) and a property ombudsperson (==2020==).

#### ii) Accountability tools
Good governance needs **sex-disaggregated data** — such as the Labour Force Survey's figure of **24.4%** female participation (==2024–25==) — and **gender-responsive budgets**, so that citizens can see whether policy serves women.

#### iii) The gaps
**No women federal ministers** in ==2025==; a **10%** job quota but few women in top grades; **Ayesha Malik** became the first woman Supreme Court judge only in ==2022==; and all-male ***jirgas*** still order *vani* and honour punishments.

### 6. Judgement

#### i) The verdict
In **Hanna Pitkin**'s terms (==1967==), quotas have given Pakistan **descriptive** representation; good governance requires **substantive** representation — policies that actually serve women. Good governance and gender equality are inseparable: without women's participation, governance fails its own tests of participation, equity, responsiveness and accountability.

#### ii) The way forward
Pakistan has built the **machinery**; it now needs the **resources and political will** — women in cabinets and senior posts, gender-responsive budgets and accountable data — to make it work.

## Why this answer scores
- Gives the **World Bank (1992)** definition and separates **governance from government**.
- Lists **UNESCAP's eight characteristics** and shows that gender is built into them.
- Answers the "**why**" with four clear arguments and the **village pump** example.
- Applies the argument to **Pakistan's machinery and gaps**; a **cycle** figure links gender to each characteristic.
